202
00:17:34,627 --> 00:17:40,147
Looking down your sights as you open the door and a guy with a shotgun blasts you in the face.

203
00:17:40,147 --> 00:17:47,508
People have always wanted a battle royale video game, but it never happened.

204
00:17:47,508 --> 00:17:51,907
First came the original teen deathmatch island Battle Royale.

205
00:17:51,907 --> 00:17:55,587
The Japanese book and movie told the story of 42 students

206
00:17:55,587 --> 00:18:00,707
dropped on a deserted island and forced to kill each other until only one was left.

207
00:18:06,579 --> 00:18:34,579
It got people asking what they would do if they were in that situation. But the movie was not released in the west for years, leaving it an obscure cult classic. Then years later, Hunger Games came around, and it reignited the fire. People wanted battle royale games, but no one was making them. But one person wanted it more than anybody else.

208
00:18:34,579 --> 00:18:37,579
Brendan, PlayerUnknown, Green

209
00:18:37,579 --> 00:19:03,579
Brendan Green was not a game designer. He was an Irish photographer and web designer who followed the love of his life to Brazil. Two years later, he was divorced and looking to get his life back on track. He made it back home to Ireland. And while living on welfare, Green discovered the games Arma and DayZ. For those who don't know, Arma is a hyper-realistic military shooter, known for the scale of its combat, its attention to detail, and graphic fiction.

210
00:19:03,579 --> 00:19:19,579
Fidelity. Also known for being the foundation for an incredible number of mods. Many of which were spun off of into their own games. DayZ is one of the best known Arma mods, which use the hyper-realistic combat mechanics to simulate a zombie apocalypse.

211
00:19:19,579 --> 00:19:35,579
So yeah, I started off making a mod basically back in ARMA 2 because I wanted to make a game I wanted to play. I saw games like the Survivor games, which are events for streamers, and I wanted to play that myself. So I decided to see if I could actually make one and ARMA 2 Battle Royale was born.

212
00:19:35,579 --> 00:19:55,579
The Battle Royale mod for ARMA 2 was a departure from the tightly directed experiences offered by AAA games like Assassin's Creed. Green was not interested in those types of games. He wanted something looser, something grittier, something that gave you the freedom to show what you would do in a battle to the death.

213
00:19:55,579 --> 00:20:04,579
As DayZ broke free of ARMA 2 and became a standalone game, other developers took notice of how popular Green's mods were in the niche ARMA community.

214
00:20:04,579 --> 00:20:13,579
Sony Online Entertainment, now known as Daybreak, hired Green as a consultant on their own DayZ-style zombie survival game.

215
00:20:13,579 --> 00:20:25,579
That mode eventually became H1Z1 King of the Kill, but by February 2016, Green had moved on. He was headed to Korea, where he got an interesting offer.

216
00:20:25,579 --> 00:20:38,579
Chang Han Kim, a Korean game designer working for Bluehole, wanted to make a Battle Royale game. He was inspired by DayZ, but when he researched the Battle Royale format more and more, he kept coming across Green.

217
00:20:38,579 --> 00:20:52,579
And less than one month later, he was Bluehole's creative director. Development moved fast. Green and Kim's team had the autonomy to create the game that they wanted. And they wanted it to be playable within one year.

218
00:20:56,756 --> 00:21:03,756
The game started its development in early 2016, and the plan was to follow an early access schedule.

